  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 332 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nNow - Sunday Night...\n\nLooking across the region this morning, temperatures have fallen \ndown into the low to mid 30s for most places, thanks in part to the \nsurface high pressure, calm winds, and good radiational cooling. \nTemperatures in the upper 20s to low 30s are still forecast, with \nthe area-wide Freeze Warning in effect through 10 AM EST. Urban \nareas will still have the biggest difficulty falling below \nfreezing, however frost development is still likely for those \nlocations. River valley fog has also developed over the past few \nhours, but should dissipate shortly after sunrise. SInce many of \nthese locations could be experiencing below freezing temperatures, \nif you are driving through dense fog, pay attention for any slick \nspots that may form on roadways.\n\nThe dry northwesterly flow aloft and surface high pressure will keep \nus dry to close out the weekend. While it will be chilly this \nmorning, temperatures Sunday afternoon will be slightly warmer than \nSaturday, with highs in the upper 50s/low 60s forecast. Clear skies \nSunday night will again allow for decent radiational cooling to take \nplace. Temperatures will not be as cold as this morning, with the \nmost recent NBM having less than 10% chances for below freezing \ntemperatures Monday morning. Lows will be in the low to mid 30s, so \nwe may end up needing to have one more Frost Advisory before the end \nof the growing season. River valley fog will also be likely Sunday \nnight, with similar conditions to this morning.\n\nMonday - Wednesday...\n\nSurface high pressure, paired with dry northwesterly flow aloft due \nto the upper-level ridge over the central CONUS, will keep us in a \ndry pattern going into the middle portions of the upcoming workweek. \nA weaker shortwave over the Red River Valley will shift eastward \ntowards the Tennessee Valley by late Tuesday, but limited moisture \nwith this wave will likely result in no rainfall occurring while it \npasses by. Surface winds will be generally light, allowing for \nmoderating temperatures. Highs on Monday will be in the low 60s, \nincreasing to the mid to upper 60s Tuesday and Wednesday. Morning \nlows will be in the upper 30s to low 40s, with the potential for \npatchy frost in some of the east central Kentucky valleys. By \nmidweek, the upper-level pattern overhead briefly shifts from a \nnorthwesterly flow regime to more of a zonal flow pattern before a \nconsiderable pattern change takes shape over the latter portions of \nthe workweek.\n\nWednesday Night - Saturday...\n\nThe late week pattern over the Ohio Valley becomes more active as a \nshortwave over the eastern Rockies/Great Plains swings eastward. As \nthis is ongoing, the polar and subtropical jets will begin to \ninteract and phase with each other, resulting in fairly quick \ncyclogenesis. This will produce a rather strong system over our area \nthat will move towards the northeast CONUS by the end of next week \nas a mature cyclone. The timing and progression of the shortwave, as \nwell as the phasing of the jets, will play a role in how much \nprecipitation we get out of this system late Wednesday through \nFriday. Many LREF members from the most recent run keep \naccumulations below an inch, with a few members outputting 1-1.5\" of \nprecip. With decent cold air being funneled in behind the system, if \nthere is enough post-frontal moisture in place over us, we could see \nsome snow flakes late Thursday into Friday. There are a few isolated \nmembers from the most recent LREF outputting light snow, so we will \nhave to see how this system trends over the coming days.\n\nAs was previously mentioned, the cold air filling in behind the low \nwill setup a late week/early weekend cool down across the Ohio \nValley. Highs Thursday through Saturday will be in the upper 40s/low \n50s, with a return to below freezing temperatures for most places \nFriday and Saturday morning. Conditions will also be dry by the \nstart of the weekend once the system fully departs off to the \nnortheast.",
